{"id":"gen-1790978346-uopjSzNvMnZ32FA0fzJd","object":"chat.completion","created":1790978346,"model":"cohere/north-mini-code:free","provider":"Cohere","system_fingerprint":null,"service_tier":null,"choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop","native_finish_reason":"complete","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-c2-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"c2-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-c2-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"c2\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            2,\n            2,\n            2,\n            0\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"e\",\n          \"f\",\n          \"g\",\n          \"h\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6412\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c2-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"c1-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c2-proposer\"\n}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"We need to solve a CSP with variables a..p, domain values {0,1,2,3}. There are constraints c1..c16 (binary constraints on pairs of variables) and also c17..c20 are \"bridges\" linking components? Actually constraints list includes c1..c16 as regular constraints, and c17..c20 appear also in bridges. But c17..c20 appear both as constraints (with allowed pairs) and as bridges linking components. The \"bridges\" list defines connections between components: c17 from c1 to c2 variables b,e; c18 from c2 to c3 variables f,i; c19 from c3 to c4 variables j,m; c20 from c4 to c1 variables n,a. So these are constraints that are perhaps required to be satisfied across components. However the constraints list already includes c17..c20 as constraints with allowed_pairs (c17 has allowed pairs [0,2], [0,3]; c18 has [2,0], [3,1]; c19 has [2,3], [0,0]; c20 has [2,1], [3,3]). So those constraints are the same as the bridge constraints.\n\nThus we need to find all satisfying assignments for component c2, which includes variables e,f,g,h. And we need to enumerate all satisfying tuples (assignments for e,f,g,h) that satisfy all constraints (including those that involve variables in other components). The constraints are:\n\nc1: (a,b) allowed pairs: [1,0], [1,3], [0,2], [2,3], [0,1], [0,3] => allowed set.\n\nc2: (b,c) allowed: [0,3], [1,1], [1,3], [1,2], [3,3], [0,2].\n\nc3: (c,d) allowed: [3,1], [0,1], [0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [1,1].\n\nc4: (d,a) allowed: [1,1], [2,1], [1,2], [2,2], [2,0], [3,2].\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed: [2,2], [1,2], [3,1], [1,3], [1,0], [2,0].\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed: [2,2], [1,1], [0,0], [1,3], [0,2], [0,3].\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed: [2,0], [3,2], [1,1], [2,1], [1,3], [3,1].\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed: [0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [2,3], [3,0], [2,1].\n\nc9: (i,j) allowed: [0,2], [2,0], [0,3], [3,2], [2,1], [2,3].\n\nc10: (j,k) allowed: [2,3], [0,0], [1,1], [2,2], [1,3], [3,0].\n\nc11: (k,l) allowed: [3,0], [0,3], [1,2], [1,0], [3,1], [3,3].\n\nc12: (l,i) allowed: [0,0], [3,3], [2,1], [2,0], [0,3], [1,3].\n\nc13: (m,n) allowed: [3,2], [2,2], [3,3], [1,1], [0,2], [1,2].\n\nc14: (n,o) allowed: [2,3], [3,2], [1,0], [1,2], [0,3], [2,2].\n\nc15: (o,p) allowed: [3,3], [1,0], [0,0], [0,1], [1,3], [3,2].\n\nc16: (p,m) allowed: [3,3], [1,0], [0,2], [1,1], [0,1], [3,0].\n\nc17: (b,e) allowed: [0,2], [0,3].\n\nc18: (f,i) allowed: [2,0], [3,1].\n\nc19: (j,m) allowed: [2,3], [0,0].\n\nc20: (n,a) allowed: [2,1], [3,3].\n\nGoal: enumerate all satisfying assignments for component c2, i.e., all possible tuples (e,f,g,h) that can be part of a full assignment satisfying all constraints.\n\nWe need to find all possible values for e,f,g,h that can be extended to full assignments for all variables a..p respecting constraints. Then enumerate all tuples for c2 (e,f,g,h) in lexicographic order (presumably based on variable order e,f,g,h). The output should be a JSON envelope with the claim's value.tuples replaced by the list of tuples.\n\nWe must not add any prose. The envelope template is given; we need to replace the placeholder \"tuples\": [ [0,0,0,0] ] with the full list.\n\nWe need to compute all possible (e,f,g,h) that are part of at least one solution.\n\nWe need to solve the CSP. There are 16 variables, domain size 4 each => 4^16 possibilities, but constraints reduce drastically.\n\nWe need to enumerate all solutions (or at least all possible e,f,g,h combos). We can attempt to solve manually via reasoning, but it's complex. However we can try to systematically search using reasoning and constraints.\n\nLet's denote variables:\n\n- a, b, c, d form component c1.\n- e, f, g, h form component c2.\n- i, j, k, l form component c3.\n- m, n, o, p form component c4.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: allowed (a,b) pairs: list1 = { (1,0), (1,3), (0,2), (2,3), (0,1), (0,3) }.\n\nc2: allowed (b,c) pairs: list2 = { (0,3), (1,1), (1,3), (1,2), (3,3), (0,2) }.\n\nc3: allowed (c,d) pairs: list3 = { (3,1), (0,1), (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (1,1) }.\n\nc4: allowed (d,a) pairs: list4 = { (1,1), (2,1), (1,2), (2,2), (2,0), (3,2) }.\n\nc5: allowed (e,f): list5 = { (2,2), (1,2), (3,1), (1,3), (1,0), (2,0) }.\n\nc6: allowed (f,g): list6 = { (2,2), (1,1), (0,0), (1,3), (0,2), (0,3) }.\n\nc7: allowed (g,h): list7 = { (2,0), (3,2), (1,1), (2,1), (1,3), (3,1) }.\n\nc8: allowed (h,e): list8 = { (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (2,3), (3,0), (2,1) }.\n\nc9: allowed (i,j): list9 = { (0,2), (2,0), (0,3), (3,2), (2,1), (2,3) }.\n\nc10: allowed (j,k): list10 = { (2,3), (0,0), (1,1), (2,2), (1,3), (3,0) }.\n\nc11: allowed (k,l): list11 = { (3,0), (0,3), (1,2), (1,0), (3,1), (3,3) }.\n\nc12: allowed (l,i): list12 = { (0,0), (3,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3) }.\n\nc13: allowed (m,n): list13 = { (3,2), (2,2), (3,3), (1,1), (0,2), (1,2) }.\n\nc14: allowed (n,o): list14 = { (2,3), (3,2), (1,0), (1,2), (0,3), (2,2) }.\n\nc15: allowed (o,p): list15 = { (3,3), (1,0), (0,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2) }.\n\nc16: allowed (p,m): list16 = { (3,3), (1,0), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1), (3,0) }.\n\nc17: allowed (b,e): list17 = { (0,2), (0,3) }.\n\nc18: allowed (f,i): list18 = { (2,0), (3,1) }.\n\nc19: allowed (j,m): list19 = { (2,3), (0,0) }.\n\nc20: allowed (n,a): list20 = { (2,1), (3,3) }.\n\nThus we have a cyclic chain of components: c1->c2->c3->c4->c1 via bridges.\n\nGoal: find all possible assignments for e,f,g,h that can be extended to a full solution.\n\nWe need to enumerate all solutions or at least all e,f,g,h combos.\n\nWe could try to solve the CSP using constraint propagation manually. But it's fairly large; however perhaps the constraints are designed to have limited solutions.\n\nWe can try to solve systematically. Let's denote the variables and constraints.\n\nWe have constraints among a,b,c,d (c1-c4). These form a cycle: a-b, b-c, c-d, d-a.\n\nThen constraints among e,f,g,h (c5-c8) form a cycle: e-f, f-g, g-h, h-e.\n\nThen constraints linking components: b-e (c17), f-i (c18), j-m (c19), n-a (c20). And also constraints among i,j,k,l (c9-c12) form a cycle: i-j, j-k, k-l, l-i.\n\nAnd constraints among m,n,o,p (c13-c16) form a cycle: m-n, n-o, o-p, p-m.\n\nThus overall we have three independent cycles: (a,b,c,d), (e,f,g,h), (i,j,k,l), (m,n,o,p). But they are linked via cross constraints: b-e, f-i, j-m, n-a. So the cycles are connected in a chain: a-b-c-d-a; e-f-g-h-e; i-j-k-l-i; m-n-o-p-m; plus cross edges linking b-e, f-i, j-m, n-a. So overall it's a single connected graph.\n\nWe need to find all assignments for all variables.\n\nWe could attempt to solve using backtracking manually, but there are many possibilities. However the constraints are limited: each binary constraint has 6 allowed pairs out of 16 possible. So each pair reduces possibilities.\n\nWe can try to solve systematically using reasoning, perhaps using constraint satisfaction solving manually with some pruning.\n\nAlternatively, we could try to write a quick mental program to enumerate all possibilities? That would be huge (4^16 ~ 4.3e9). But constraints reduce drastically.\n\nWe can try to use logical deduction to reduce possibilities for each variable.\n\nApproach: treat constraints as a graph, maybe we can find all solutions via reasoning.\n\nLet's start with the cross constraints that have only 2 allowed pairs each (c17-c20). Those are more restrictive.\n\nc17: (b,e) allowed pairs: (0,2) or (0,3). So b must be 0, and e must be either 2 or 3. So b=0, e ∈ {2,3}.\n\nc18: (f,i) allowed: (2,0) or (3,1). So f ∈ {2,3}, i accordingly: if f=2 then i=0; if f=3 then i=1.\n\nc19: (j,m) allowed: (2,3) or (0,0). So j ∈ {2,0}, m accordingly: if j=2 then m=3; if j=0 then m=0.\n\nc20: (n,a) allowed: (2,1) or (3,3). So n ∈ {2,3}, a accordingly: if n=2 then a=1; if n=3 then a=3.\n\nThus we have strong constraints linking these variables.\n\nNow we also have constraints within each component.\n\nLet's start with component c1: a,b,c,d.\n\nWe know b=0 from c17. So b is fixed to 0.\n\nNow c1 constraint c1: (a,b) allowed pairs: b=0, a must be such that (a,0) is allowed. List c1 allowed pairs: (1,0), (1,3), (0,2), (2,3), (0,1), (0,3). So we need (a,0) in that set. Let's see which a values appear with b=0: (1,0) => a=1; (0,2) => b=2 not 0; (2,3) => b=3; (0,1) => b=1; (0,3) => b=3; (1,3) => b=3. So only (1,0) has b=0. So a must be 1. So a=1.\n\nThus from c17 and c1, we have a=1, b=0.\n\nNow c4: (d,a) allowed pairs: a=1, so (d,1) must be allowed. List c4 allowed: (1,1), (2,1), (1,2), (2,2), (2,0), (3,2). So we need (d,1) in that set. Which entries have second component 1? (1,1) => d=1; (2,1) => d=2. So d ∈ {1,2}.\n\nThus d ∈ {1,2}.\n\nNow c2: (b,c) allowed pairs: b=0, so (0,c) must be allowed. List c2 allowed: (0,3), (1,1), (1,3), (1,2), (3,3), (0,2). So (0,c) appears for c=3 (0,3) and c=2 (0,2). So c ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus c ∈ {2,3}.\n\nNow c3: (c,d) allowed pairs: we need to consider possible c values (2 or 3) and d values (1 or 2). Let's list allowed pairs for c3: (3,1), (0,1), (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (1,1). So allowed combos:\n\n- (c,d) = (3,1) is allowed.\n- (c,d) = (0,1) but c=0 not allowed.\n- (c,d) = (0,2) c=0 not allowed.\n- (c,d) = (1,0) c=1 not allowed.\n- (c,d) = (1,3) c=1 not allowed.\n- (c,d) = (1,1) c=1 not allowed.\n\nThus only possible (c,d) from allowed list that matches c ∈ {2,3} and d ∈ {1,2} is (c,d) = (3,1). So c=3, d=1.\n\nThus c is forced to 3, d forced to 1.\n\nThus component c1 is fully determined: a=1, b=0, c=3, d=1.\n\nNow check all constraints for c1 are satisfied: c1 (a,b) = (1,0) allowed; c2 (b,c) = (0,3) allowed; c3 (c,d) = (3,1) allowed; c4 (d,a) = (1,1) allowed (since (1,1) is allowed). So component c1 is consistent.\n\nNow we have constraints linking to other components: c17 (b,e) already used: b=0, e ∈ {2,3}. c20 (n,a): a=1, so (n,1) must be allowed. c20 allowed pairs: (2,1) or (3,3). So (n,a) = (n,1) must be (2,1) because a=1. So n must be 2. So n=2. That also satisfies c20: (2,1) is allowed.\n\nThus n=2.\n\nNow c13: (m,n) allowed: n=2, so (m,2) must be allowed. List c13 allowed pairs: (3,2), (2,2), (3,3), (1,1), (0,2), (1,2). So we need second component 2. Which pairs have second component 2? (3,2) => m=3; (2,2) => m=2; (0,2) => m=0; (1,2) => m=1. So m ∈ {0,1,2,3}? Actually all four values appear: 3,2,0,1. So m can be 0,1,2,3. So m is unrestricted by c13 given n=2.\n\nNow c14: (n,o) allowed: n=2, so (2,o) must be allowed. List c14 allowed: (2,3), (3,2), (1,0), (1,2), (0,3), (2,2). So (2,o) appears for o=3 (2,3) and o=2 (2,2). So o ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus o ∈ {2,3}.\n\nNow c15: (o,p) allowed: depends on o.\n\nc16: (p,m) allowed: depends on p and m.\n\nNow component c2: e,f,g,h.\n\nWe have b=0, c17: (b,e) = (0,e) must be allowed: allowed pairs (0,2) or (0,3). So e ∈ {2,3} as earlier.\n\nThus e ∈ {2,3}.\n\nNow c5: (e,f) allowed: depends on e.\n\nc5 allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,2), (3,1), (1,3), (1,0), (2,0). So for each e, possible f values:\n\n- If e=2: allowed (2,2) => f=2; (2,0) => f=0. So f ∈ {0,2} when e=2.\n\n- If e=3: allowed (3,1) => f=1. No other with e=3? Check list: (3,1) only. So if e=3, f must be 1.\n\nThus possibilities:\n\nCase 1: e=2, f ∈ {0,2}.\n\nCase 2: e=3, f=1.\n\nNow c6: (f,g) allowed: depends on f.\n\nc6 allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,1), (0,0), (1,3), (0,2), (0,3). So for each f:\n\n- If f=0: allowed (0,0) => g=0; (0,2) => g=2; (0,3) => g=3. So g ∈ {0,2,3}.\n\n- If f=1: allowed (1,1) => g=1; (1,3) => g=3. So g ∈ {1,3}.\n\n- If f=2: allowed (2,2) => g=2. Also maybe (2,?) other? No other with first component 2. So g=2.\n\nThus g possibilities depend on f.\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed: depends on g.\n\nc7 allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,2), (1,1), (2,1), (1,3), (3,1). So for each g:\n\n- If g=0: no allowed pair with first component 0. So g cannot be 0. So any assignment with g=0 is invalid. So we must avoid g=0.\n\n- If g=1: allowed (1,1) => h=1; (1,3) => h=3. So h ∈ {1,3}.\n\n- If g=2: allowed (2,0) => h=0; (2,1) => h=1. So h ∈ {0,1}.\n\n- If g=3: allowed (3,2) => h=2; (3,1) => h=1. So h ∈ {1,2}.\n\nThus g cannot be 0; possible g values are 1,2,3.\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed: depends on h and e.\n\nc8 allowed pairs: (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (2,3), (3,0), (2,1). So for each e, possible h values:\n\n- If e=2: allowed pairs with second component 2? Actually (h,e) means first is h, second is e. So we need (h,2) in allowed list. Check allowed list:\n\n(0,2) => h=0.\n(1,0) => e=0 not relevant.\n(1,3) => e=3 not relevant.\n(2,3) => e=3.\n(3,0) => e=0.\n(2,1) => e=1.\n\nThus only (0,2) has e=2. So if e=2, h must be 0.\n\n- If e=3: allowed pairs with e=3: (1,3) => h=1; (2,3) => h=2. So if e=3, h ∈ {1,2}.\n\nThus h possibilities:\n\n- If e=2: h=0.\n- If e=3: h ∈ {1,2}.\n\nNow combine with earlier constraints on h from c7.\n\nThus we need to satisfy both c7 (g->h) and c8 (h,e) constraints.\n\nThus we can enumerate possibilities for e,f,g,h.\n\nWe also have cross constraints linking f to i (c18) and e to b (c17 already used). So f determines i via c18. And i then participates in component c3 constraints (i,j,k,l). So f influences i.\n\nThus we need to consider f possibilities.\n\nThus let's enumerate possibilities for e,f,g,h systematically.\n\nWe have two cases for e:\n\nCase A: e=2.\n\n- Then b=0, c17 satisfied (0,2) allowed.\n\n- c5: (e,f) allowed: e=2 => f ∈ {0,2}.\n\n- c8: h must be 0 (since e=2 => h=0). So h=0.\n\n- c7: (g,h) = (g,0) must be allowed. Allowed pairs with second component 0: from c7 list: (2,0) => g=2, h=0; (3,2) not; (1,1) not; (2,1) not; (1,3) not; (3,1) not. So only (2,0) is allowed for (g,h) with h=0. So g must be 2. So g=2.\n\nThus for e=2, we have forced: h=0, g=2.\n\nNow c6: (f,g) = (f,2) must be allowed. c6 allowed pairs: (2,2) => f=2; (1,3) => g=3; (0,2) => f=0,g=2; (0,3) => g=3; (1,1) => g=1; (0,0) => g=0. So only (0,2) and (2,2) have second component 2. So f can be 0 (if (0,2)) or 2 (if (2,2)). Both are allowed.\n\nThus f ∈ {0,2} as already from c5.\n\nThus for e=2, we have:\n\n- h=0, g=2.\n\n- f ∈ {0,2}.\n\n- e=2.\n\nThus possible (e,f,g,h) combos:\n\n1. (2,0,2,0)\n2. (2,2,2,0)\n\nNow we need to check other constraints: c8 is satisfied (h=0,e=2) allowed (0,2). c7 satisfied (g=2,h=0) allowed (2,0). c6 satisfied for f choices: (0,2) allowed; (2,2) allowed. c5 satisfied for e=2: (2,0) allowed; (2,2) allowed. c17 satisfied (b=0,e=2) allowed. So component c2 constraints are satisfied for both combos.\n\nNow we need to ensure cross constraints linking f to i (c18) and other components can be satisfied.\n\nc18: (f,i) allowed: if f=0, then (0,i) must be allowed. c18 allowed pairs: (2,0) and (3,1). So (0,i) is not allowed for any i (since only (2,0) and (3,1)). So f cannot be 0 because (0,i) not allowed. Therefore f must be 2. So f=2 is forced.\n\nThus for e=2 case, we must have f=2. So (e,f,g,h) = (2,2,2,0) is the only viable tuple.\n\nThus one tuple for c2: (e=2, f=2, g=2, h=0).\n\nNow check c18: f=2 => i=0 (since (2,0) allowed). So i=0.\n\nThus i is forced to 0.\n\nNow we need to satisfy component c3 constraints: i,j,k,l with i=0.\n\nComponent c3 constraints:\n\nc9: (i,j) allowed: i=0 => (0,j) must be allowed. c9 allowed pairs: (0,2), (2,0), (0,3), (3,2), (2,1), (2,3). So (0,j) appears for j=2 (0,2) and j=3 (0,3). So j ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus j ∈ {2,3}.\n\nc10: (j,k) allowed: depends on j.\n\nc10 allowed pairs: (2,3), (0,0), (1,1), (2,2), (1,3), (3,0). So for each possible j:\n\n- If j=2: allowed (2,3) => k=3; (2,2) => k=2. So k ∈ {2,3}.\n\n- If j=3: allowed (3,0) => k=0. Also maybe (j,k) = (3,?) other? No other with first component 3. So if j=3, k=0.\n\nThus possibilities:\n\n- j=2 => k ∈ {2,3}\n- j=3 => k=0\n\nc11: (k,l) allowed: depends on k.\n\nc11 allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,3), (1,2), (1,0), (3,1), (3,3). So for each possible k:\n\n- If k=2: no pair with first component 2. So k cannot be 2. So j cannot be 2 with k=2? Actually we need to consider that k must be allowed with l; if k=2, there is no allowed (2,l). So k cannot be 2. So if j=2, k must be 3 (since k ∈ {2,3} but 2 invalid). So j=2 => k=3.\n\n- If k=3: allowed (3,0) => l=0; (3,1) => l=1; (3,3) => l=3. So l ∈ {0,1,3}.\n\n- If k=0: allowed (0,3) => l=3. So l=3.\n\nThus we have constraints:\n\n- j=2 => k=3 => l ∈ {0,1,3}\n- j=3 => k=0 => l=3\n\nNow c12: (l,i) allowed: i=0, so (l,0) must be allowed. c12 allowed pairs: (0,0), (3,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3). So (l,0) appears for l=0 (0,0) and l=2 (2,0). So l ∈ {0,2}.\n\nThus l must be 0 or 2.\n\nNow combine with earlier possibilities for l:\n\n- If j=2 => k=3 => l ∈ {0,1,3} from c11. Intersection with l ∈ {0,2} yields l=0 only (since 1,3 not allowed). So l=0.\n\n- If j=3 => k=0 => l=3 from c11. Intersection with l ∈ {0,2} yields no solution because l=3 not allowed. So j=3 case is invalid.\n\nThus only j=2 works, with k=3, l=0.\n\nThus we have determined component c3: i=0, j=2, k=3, l=0.\n\nNow check all constraints for c3:\n\nc9 (i,j) = (0,2) allowed.\nc10 (j,k) = (2,3) allowed.\nc11 (k,l) = (3,0) allowed.\nc12 (l,i) = (0,0) allowed.\n\nThus component c3 is consistent.\n\nNow we have cross constraints linking j to m (c19) and i to f (c18 already used). c19: (j,m) allowed: j=2 => (2,m) must be allowed. c19 allowed pairs: (2,3) and (0,0). So (2,m) allowed only for m=3. So m=3.\n\nThus m=3.\n\nNow component c4: variables m,n,o,p.\n\nWe already have n=2, m=3, o ∈ {2,3} (from earlier). Also p must satisfy constraints with o (c15) and m (c16). Let's enumerate.\n\nWe have m=3, n=2, o ∈ {2,3}.\n\nc13: (m,n) = (3,2) allowed? Check c13 allowed pairs: (3,2) is present. So ok.\n\nc14: (n,o) = (2,o) allowed: we already considered o ∈ {2,3} because (2,2) and (2,3) allowed.\n\nc15: (o,p) allowed: depends on o.\n\nc15 allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2). So for each o:\n\n- If o=2: allowed (3,2) => p=2? Wait (3,2) is (o,p) = (3,2) but o=2 not 3. Actually (3,2) means o=3, p=2. So not applicable. Also (o,p) = (2,p) appears? Check list: (3,3) => o=3,p=3; (1,0) => o=1,p=0; (0,0) => o=0,p=0; (0,1) => o=0,p=1; (1,3) => o=1,p=3; (3,2) => o=3,p=2. So there is no pair with o=2. So o cannot be 2 because no allowed (2,p). So o must be 3.\n\nThus o=3.\n\nThus o=3.\n\nNow c15: (o,p) = (3,p) must be allowed. Allowed pairs with o=3: (3,3) => p=3; (3,2) => p=2. So p ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus p ∈ {2,3}.\n\nNow c16: (p,m) allowed: m=3, so (p,3) must be allowed. c16 allowed pairs: (3,3), (1,0), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1), (3,0). So (p,3) appears only for p=3? Let's check: (3,3) => p=3; (1,0) => second component 0; (0,2) => second component 2; (1,1) => second component 1; (0,1) => second component 1; (3,0) => second component 0. So only (3,3) has second component 3. So p must be 3.\n\nThus p=3.\n\nThus p=3.\n\nThus p ∈ {2,3} narrowed to p=3.\n\nNow check c15: (o,p) = (3,3) allowed (yes). So consistent.\n\nThus component c4 is fully determined: m=3, n=2, o=3, p=3.\n\nNow check all constraints for component c4:\n\nc13 (m,n) = (3,2) allowed.\nc14 (n,o) = (2,3) allowed.\nc15 (o,p) = (3,3) allowed.\nc16 (p,m) = (3,3) allowed.\n\nAll good.\n\nNow we have all variables assigned:\n\n- a=1\n- b=0\n- c=3\n- d=1\n- e=2\n- f=2\n- g=2\n- h=0\n- i=0\n- j=2\n- k=3\n- l=0\n- m=3\n- n=2\n- o=3\n- p=3\n\nNow we need to verify all constraints for the entire CSP to ensure consistency.\n\nLet's list each constraint and verify:\n\nc1 (a,b) = (1,0) allowed? Yes (1,0) is in allowed list.\n\nc2 (b,c) = (0,3) allowed? Yes (0,3) is in list.\n\nc3 (c,d) = (3,1) allowed? Yes (3,1) is in list.\n\nc4 (d,a) = (1,1) allowed? Yes (1,1) is in list.\n\nc5 (e,f) = (2,2) allowed? Yes (2,2) is in list.\n\nc6 (f,g) = (2,2) allowed? Yes (2,2) is in list.\n\nc7 (g,h) = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is in list.\n\nc8 (h,e) = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) is in list.\n\nc9 (i,j) = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) is in list.\n\nc10 (j,k) = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) is in list.\n\nc11 (k,l) = (3,0) allowed? Yes (3,0) is in list.\n\nc12 (l,i) = (0,0) allowed? Yes (0,0) is in list.\n\nc13 (m,n) = (3,2) allowed? Yes (3,2) is in list.\n\nc14 (n,o) = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) is in list.\n\nc15 (o,p) = (3,3) allowed? Yes (3,3) is in list.\n\nc16 (p,m) = (3,3) allowed? Yes (3,3) is in list.\n\nc17 (b,e) = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) is in list.\n\nc18 (f,i) = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is in list.\n\nc19 (j,m) = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) is in list.\n\nc20 (n,a) = (2,1) allowed? Yes (2,1) is in list.\n\nThus this is a consistent full assignment.\n\nThus we have found one solution. However we need to enumerate all satisfying assignments for component c2 (e,f,g,h). There may be other solutions besides the one we found.\n\nWe need to find all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that can be extended to a full solution. So far we found one: (2,2,2,0). There may be other possibilities, maybe with e=3 case.\n\nThus we need to explore other possibilities.\n\nWe need to systematically explore all possible assignments for e,f,g,h that satisfy constraints and can be extended to a full solution.\n\nWe already explored e=2 case, which forced f=2 (due to c18), g=2, h=0. That gave one solution.\n\nNow we need to explore e=3 case.\n\nCase B: e=3.\n\n- c17: (b,e) = (0,3) allowed? Yes (0,3) is allowed. So b=0, e=3 is okay.\n\n- c5: (e,f) = (3,f) must be allowed. c5 allowed pairs: (3,1) is the only one with e=3. So f=1.\n\nThus f=1.\n\n- c8: (h,e) = (h,3) must be allowed. c8 allowed pairs: (1,3) and (2,3) have second component 3. So h ∈ {1,2}.\n\nThus h ∈ {1,2}.\n\n- c6: (f,g) = (1,g) must be allowed. c6 allowed pairs: (1,1) and (1,3) have first component 1. So g ∈ {1,3}.\n\nThus g ∈ {1,3}.\n\n- c7: (g,h) must be allowed. Let's consider possible combos of g ∈ {1,3} and h ∈ {1,2}.\n\nc7 allowed pairs: (1,1), (1,3), (2,0), (2,1), (3,2), (3,1). So:\n\n- If g=1: allowed h=1 or h=3. But h ∈ {1,2}, so h can be 1 (allowed) but h=2 not allowed with g=1. So g=1 => h must be 1.\n\n- If g=3: allowed h=2 or h=1. So g=3 => h can be 1 or 2 (both allowed). So g=3 => h ∈ {1,2}.\n\nThus possible combos:\n\n- (g=1, h=1)\n- (g=3, h=1)\n- (g=3, h=2)\n\nNow we need to check c8: (h,e) = (h,3) must be allowed. For each h:\n\n- h=1: (1,3) allowed (yes).\n- h=2: (2,3) allowed (yes).\n\nThus both h=1 and h=2 are allowed with e=3.\n\nThus all three combos are allowed for (g,h) given constraints.\n\nNow we have e=3, f=1, g ∈ {1,3}, h ∈ {1,2} with constraints above.\n\nThus possible (e,f,g,h) combos:\n\n1. (3,1,1,1)\n2. (3,1,3,1)\n3. (3,1,3,2)\n\nNow we need to check cross constraints: c18 (f,i) = (1,i) must be allowed. c18 allowed pairs: (2,0) and (3,1). So (1,i) is not allowed for any i. So f=1 is invalid! Indeed c18 only allows f=2 or f=3. So f cannot be 1. Therefore e=3 case is impossible because f=1 violates c18.\n\nThus e=3 case yields no solution.\n\nThus the only possible e value is 2, and we found only one solution for c2.\n\nBut we must double-check if there might be other possibilities for e=2 case besides (2,2,2,0). We earlier deduced that e=2 forces b=0, c17 satisfied, c5 allowed f ∈ {0,2}, c8 forced h=0, c7 forced g=2, c6 allowed f ∈ {0,2}, c18 forced f=2. So indeed f must be 2. So (2,2,2,0) is the only possible tuple for e=2.\n\nThus there is exactly one solution for component c2.\n\nBut we must verify that there aren't other possibilities for e=2 case where maybe g could be something else? Let's re-express constraints for e=2 case.\n\nWe had:\n\n- e=2.\n\n- c5: (e,f) allowed: (2,2) and (2,0). So f ∈ {0,2}.\n\n- c8: (h,e) allowed: (0,2) only, so h=0.\n\n- c7: (g,h) = (g,0) allowed: only (2,0) is allowed. So g=2.\n\nThus g forced to 2.\n\n- c6: (f,g) = (f,2) allowed: (0,2) and (2,2) allowed. So f ∈ {0,2} as before.\n\n- c18: (f,i) allowed: f must be 2 or 3. So f cannot be 0. So f=2.\n\nThus indeed only one solution.\n\nThus component c2 has exactly one satisfying tuple: (e=2,f=2,g=2,h=0).\n\nThus the answer is a JSON envelope with component c2 tuples containing that tuple.\n\nBut the problem says \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component c2, sorted lexicographically. Edit only payload.claim.value.tuples in the envelope. Each row contains exactly four domain integers in the listed variable order.\"\n\nThus we need to output the envelope with the claim's value.tuples set to an array containing the tuple(s). Since there is only one tuple, the array will have one element: [ [2,2,2,0] ].\n\nBut we need to ensure the order of variables is as per component c2: \"variables\": [\"e\",\"f\",\"g\",\"h\"] as per envelope template. So tuple [2,2,2,0] corresponds to e=2,f=2,g=2,h=0.\n\nThus we need to output the envelope JSON with that tuple.\n\nBut we need to be careful: The envelope template includes a \"payload.claim.value.tuples\": [ [0,0,0,0] ] placeholder. We need to replace that with the correct list.\n\nThus final output JSON should be exactly the envelope with updated \"tuples\". The rest of the envelope remains unchanged.\n\nThus we need to produce:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-c2-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"c2-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-c2-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"c2\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            2,\n            2,\n            2,\n            0\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"e\",\n          \"f\",\n          \"g\",\n          \"h\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6412\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c2-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"c1-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c2-proposer\"\n}\n\nWe must ensure no extra whitespace? The problem says output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }. So we should output exactly that JSON, with no extra spaces? Usually whitespace is okay but we can keep minimal formatting. However the template includes line breaks and spaces; we can output exactly as above, but we need to ensure we don't add extra spaces or line breaks? Usually JSON can have whitespace. The problem says \"no prose outside JSON\" and \"output raw unformatted JSON\". It may expect a compact JSON without pretty printing. However the template includes pretty printed JSON with indentation. Usually it's okay to output pretty printed JSON as long as it's valid JSON. But the phrase \"raw unformatted JSON\" might mean no extra markup, but whitespace is fine. However to be safe, we can output a compact JSON without line breaks. But the template includes line breaks; the instruction says \"Edit only payload.claim.value.tuples in the envelope.\" So we can keep the rest exactly as in template, just replace the tuples.\n\nThus we can output the envelope exactly as the template but with updated tuples. The template includes line breaks and indentation. So we can output that.\n\nThus final answer is the JSON envelope with the tuple.\n\nBefore finalizing, double-check that there are no other solutions for component c2. Let's double-check all constraints thoroughly to ensure we didn't miss any possibilities.\n\nWe should also consider if there could be alternative solutions where b is not forced to 0? Wait c17 forced b=0 because allowed pairs are (0,2) and (0,3). So b must be 0. So b is fixed.\n\nThus a is forced to 1 via c1. So a is fixed.\n\nThus n is forced to 2 via c20. So n fixed.\n\nThus many variables become forced.\n\nThus it's plausible that the CSP has a unique solution overall. Let's see if any other solutions exist by exploring alternative branches that we might have missed.\n\nWe should systematically explore the CSP to confirm uniqueness.\n\nWe have deduced b=0, a=1, n=2, i=0, j=2, k=3, l=0, m=3, o=3, p=3. That seems forced.\n\nBut we need to verify that there are no alternative possibilities for variables earlier that could lead to different assignments for e,f,g,h.\n\nWe deduced e=2 from c17 and c1? Actually c17 gave b=0, e ∈ {2,3}. So e could be 2 or 3. We explored both.\n\nWe found e=2 leads to a solution. e=3 leads to f=1 which violates c18. So e=3 is impossible.\n\nThus e=2 is forced.\n\nThus e=2 forced.\n\nNow c5 gave f ∈ {0,2}. c6 gave g possibilities based on f. c7 gave constraints linking g and h. c8 gave h forced to 0. So g forced to 2. So g forced.\n\nThus g forced.\n\nThus f must be 2 due to c18. So f forced.\n\nThus indeed e,f,g,h are forced uniquely.\n\nThus component c2 has exactly one satisfying tuple.\n\nThus answer is correct.\n\nNow we need to output the envelope.\n\nOne nuance: The envelope includes \"read_refs\" referencing \"claim:c2-tuples\" revision 0 state_revision 0. That's fine.\n\nThus final output is the envelope with the tuple.\n\nWe need to ensure the JSON is valid. Let's construct the envelope exactly as template but with updated tuple.\n\nThe template:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-c2-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"c2-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-c2-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"c2\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            0,\n            0,\n            0,\n            0\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"e\",\n          \"f\",\n          \"g\",\n          \"h\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6412\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c2-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"c1-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c2-proposer\"\n}\n\nWe need to replace the placeholder tuple with [ [2,2,2,0] ].\n\nThus final answer.\n\nNow ensure no extra prose. Output raw JSON.\n\nThus final answer is the JSON envelope.\n\nProceed.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to solve a CSP with variables a..p, domain values {0,1,2,3}. There are constraints c1..c16 (binary constraints on pairs of variables) and also c17..c20 are \"bridges\" linking components? Actually constraints list includes c1..c16 as regular constraints, and c17..c20 appear also in bridges. But c17..c20 appear both as constraints (with allowed pairs) and as bridges linking components. The \"bridges\" list defines connections between components: c17 from c1 to c2 variables b,e; c18 from c2 to c3 variables f,i; c19 from c3 to c4 variables j,m; c20 from c4 to c1 variables n,a. So these are constraints that are perhaps required to be satisfied across components. However the constraints list already includes c17..c20 as constraints with allowed_pairs (c17 has allowed pairs [0,2], [0,3]; c18 has [2,0], [3,1]; c19 has [2,3], [0,0]; c20 has [2,1], [3,3]). So those constraints are the same as the bridge constraints.\n\nThus we need to find all satisfying assignments for component c2, which includes variables e,f,g,h. And we need to enumerate all satisfying tuples (assignments for e,f,g,h) that satisfy all constraints (including those that involve variables in other components). The constraints are:\n\nc1: (a,b) allowed pairs: [1,0], [1,3], [0,2], [2,3], [0,1], [0,3] => allowed set.\n\nc2: (b,c) allowed: [0,3], [1,1], [1,3], [1,2], [3,3], [0,2].\n\nc3: (c,d) allowed: [3,1], [0,1], [0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [1,1].\n\nc4: (d,a) allowed: [1,1], [2,1], [1,2], [2,2], [2,0], [3,2].\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed: [2,2], [1,2], [3,1], [1,3], [1,0], [2,0].\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed: [2,2], [1,1], [0,0], [1,3], [0,2], [0,3].\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed: [2,0], [3,2], [1,1], [2,1], [1,3], [3,1].\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed: [0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [2,3], [3,0], [2,1].\n\nc9: (i,j) allowed: [0,2], [2,0], [0,3], [3,2], [2,1], [2,3].\n\nc10: (j,k) allowed: [2,3], [0,0], [1,1], [2,2], [1,3], [3,0].\n\nc11: (k,l) allowed: [3,0], [0,3], [1,2], [1,0], [3,1], [3,3].\n\nc12: (l,i) allowed: [0,0], [3,3], [2,1], [2,0], [0,3], [1,3].\n\nc13: (m,n) allowed: [3,2], [2,2], [3,3], [1,1], [0,2], [1,2].\n\nc14: (n,o) allowed: [2,3], [3,2], [1,0], [1,2], [0,3], [2,2].\n\nc15: (o,p) allowed: [3,3], [1,0], [0,0], [0,1], [1,3], [3,2].\n\nc16: (p,m) allowed: [3,3], [1,0], [0,2], [1,1], [0,1], [3,0].\n\nc17: (b,e) allowed: [0,2], [0,3].\n\nc18: (f,i) allowed: [2,0], [3,1].\n\nc19: (j,m) allowed: [2,3], [0,0].\n\nc20: (n,a) allowed: [2,1], [3,3].\n\nGoal: enumerate all satisfying assignments for component c2, i.e., all possible tuples (e,f,g,h) that can be part of a full assignment satisfying all constraints.\n\nWe need to find all possible values for e,f,g,h that can be extended to full assignments for all variables a..p respecting constraints. Then enumerate all tuples for c2 (e,f,g,h) in lexicographic order (presumably based on variable order e,f,g,h). The output should be a JSON envelope with the claim's value.tuples replaced by the list of tuples.\n\nWe must not add any prose. The envelope template is given; we need to replace the placeholder \"tuples\": [ [0,0,0,0] ] with the full list.\n\nWe need to compute all possible (e,f,g,h) that are part of at least one solution.\n\nWe need to solve the CSP. There are 16 variables, domain size 4 each => 4^16 possibilities, but constraints reduce drastically.\n\nWe need to enumerate all solutions (or at least all possible e,f,g,h combos). We can attempt to solve manually via reasoning, but it's complex. However we can try to systematically search using reasoning and constraints.\n\nLet's denote variables:\n\n- a, b, c, d form component c1.\n- e, f, g, h form component c2.\n- i, j, k, l form component c3.\n- m, n, o, p form component c4.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: allowed (a,b) pairs: list1 = { (1,0), (1,3), (0,2), (2,3), (0,1), (0,3) }.\n\nc2: allowed (b,c) pairs: list2 = { (0,3), (1,1), (1,3), (1,2), (3,3), (0,2) }.\n\nc3: allowed (c,d) pairs: list3 = { (3,1), (0,1), (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (1,1) }.\n\nc4: allowed (d,a) pairs: list4 = { (1,1), (2,1), (1,2), (2,2), (2,0), (3,2) }.\n\nc5: allowed (e,f): list5 = { (2,2), (1,2), (3,1), (1,3), (1,0), (2,0) }.\n\nc6: allowed (f,g): list6 = { (2,2), (1,1), (0,0), (1,3), (0,2), (0,3) }.\n\nc7: allowed (g,h): list7 = { (2,0), (3,2), (1,1), (2,1), (1,3), (3,1) }.\n\nc8: allowed (h,e): list8 = { (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (2,3), (3,0), (2,1) }.\n\nc9: allowed (i,j): list9 = { (0,2), (2,0), (0,3), (3,2), (2,1), (2,3) }.\n\nc10: allowed (j,k): list10 = { (2,3), (0,0), (1,1), (2,2), (1,3), (3,0) }.\n\nc11: allowed (k,l): list11 = { (3,0), (0,3), (1,2), (1,0), (3,1), (3,3) }.\n\nc12: allowed (l,i): list12 = { (0,0), (3,3), (2,1), (2,0), (0,3), (1,3) }.\n\nc13: allowed (m,n): list13 = { (3,2), (2,2), (3,3), (1,1), (0,2), (1,2) }.\n\nc14: allowed (n,o): list14 = { (2,3), (3,2), (1,0), (1,2), (0,3), (2,2) }.\n\nc15: allowed (o,p): list15 = { (3,3), (1,0), (0,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2) }.\n\nc16: allowed (p,m): list16 = { (3,3), (1,0), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1), (3,0) }.\n\nc17: allowed (b,e): list17 = { (0,2), (0,3) }.\n\nc18: allowed (f,i): list18 = { (2,0), (3,1) }.\n\nc19: allowed (j,m): list19 = { (2,3), (0,0) }.\n\nc20: allowed (n,a): list20 = { (2,1), (3,3) }.\n\nThus we have a cyclic chain of components: c1->c2->c3->c4->c1 via bridges.\n\nGoal: find all possible assignments for e,f,g,h that can be extended to a full solution.\n\nWe need to enumerate all solutions or at least all e,f,g,h combos.\n\nWe could try to solve the CSP using constraint propagation manually. But it's fairly large; however perhaps the constraints are designed to have limited solutions.\n\nWe can try to solve systematically. Let's denote the variables and constraints.\n\nWe have constraints among a,b,c,d (c1-c4). These form a cycle: a-b, b-c, c-d, d-a.\n\nThen constraints among e,f,g,h (c5-c8) form a cycle: e-f, f-g, g-h, h-e.\n\nThen constraints linking components: b-e (c17), f-i (c18), j-m (c19), n-a (c20). And also constraints among i,j,k,l (c9-c12) form a cycle: i-j, j-k, k-l, l-i.\n\nAnd constraints among m,n,o,p (c13-c16) form a cycle: m-n, n-o, o-p, p-m.\n\nThus overall we have three independent cycles: (a,b,c,d), (e,f,g,h), (i,j,k,l), (m,n,o,p). But they are linked via cross constraints: b-e, f-i, j-m, n-a. So the cycles are connected in a chain: a-b-c-d-a; e-f-g-h-e; i-j-k-l-i; m-n-o-p-m; plus cross edges linking b-e, f-i, j-m, n-a. So overall it's a single connected graph.\n\nWe need to find all assignments for all variables.\n\nWe could attempt to solve using backtracking manually, but there are many possibilities. However the constraints are limited: each binary constraint has 6 allowed pairs out of 16 possible. So each pair reduces possibilities.\n\nWe can try to solve systematically using reasoning, perhaps using constraint satisfaction solving manually with some pruning.\n\nAlternatively, we could try to write a quick mental program to enumerate all possibilities? That would be huge (4^16 ~ 4.3e9). But constraints reduce drastically.\n\nWe can try to use logical deduction to reduce possibilities for each variable.\n\nApproach: treat constraints as a graph, maybe we can find all solutions via reasoning.\n\nLet's start with the cross constraints that have only 2 allowed pairs each (c17-c20). Those are more restrictive.\n\nc17: (b,e) allowed pairs: (0,2) or (0,3). So b must be 0, and e must be either 2 or 3. So b=0, e ∈ {2,3}.\n\nc18: (f,i) allowed: (2,0) or (3,1). So f ∈ {2,3}, i accordingly: if f=2 then i=0; if f=3 then i=1.\n\nc19: (j,m) allowed: (2,3) or (0,0). So j ∈ {2,0}, m accordingly: if j=2 then m=3; if j=0 then m=0.\n\nc20: (n,a) allowed: (2,1) or (3,3). So n ∈ {2,3}, a accordingly: if n=2 then a=1; if n=3 then a=3.\n\nThus we have strong constraints linking these variables.\n\nNow we also have constraints within each component.\n\nLet's start with component c1: a,b,c,d.\n\nWe know b=0 from c17. So b is fixed to 0.\n\nNow c1 constraint c1: (a,b) allowed pairs: b=0, a must be such that (a,0) is allowed. List c1 allowed pairs: (1,0), (1,3), (0,2), (2,3), (0,1), (0,3). So we need (a,0) in that set. Let's see which a values appear with b=0: (1,0) => a=1; (0,2) => b=2 not 0; (2,3) => b=3; (0,1) => b=1; (0,3) => b=3; (1,3) => b=3. So only (1,0) has b=0. So a must be 1. So a=1.\n\nThus from c17 and c1, we have a=1, b=0.\n\nNow c4: (d,a) allowed pairs: a=1, so (d,1) must be allowed. List c4 allowed: (1,1), (2,1), (1,2), (2,2), (2,0), (3,2). So we need (d,1) in that set. Which entries have second component 1? (1,1) => d=1; (2,1) => d=2. So d ∈ {1,2}.\n\nThus d ∈ {1,2}.\n\nNow c2: (b,c) allowed pairs: b=0, so (0,c) must be allowed. List c2 allowed: (0,3), (1,1), (1,3), (1,2), (3,3), (0,2). So (0,c) appears for c=3 (0,3) and c=2 (0,2). So c ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus c ∈ {2,3}.\n\nNow c3: (c,d) allowed pairs: we need to consider possible c values (2 or 3) and d values (1 or 2). Let's list allowed pairs for c3: (3,1), (0,1), (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (1,1). So allowed combos:\n\n- (c,d) = (3,1) is allowed.\n- (c,d) = (0,1) but c=0 not allowed.\n- (c,d) = (0,2) c=0 not allowed.\n- (c,d) = (1,0) c=1 not allowed.\n- (c,d) = (1,3) c=1 not allowed.\n- (c,d) = (1,1) c=1 not allowed.\n\nThus only possible (c,d) from allowed list that matches c ∈ {2,3} and d ∈ {1,2} is (c,d) = (3,1). So c=3, d=1.\n\nThus c is forced to 3, d forced to 1.\n\nThus component c1 is fully determined: a=1, b=0, c=3, d=1.\n\nNow check all constraints for c1 are satisfied: c1 (a,b) = (1,0) allowed; c2 (b,c) = (0,3) allowed; c3 (c,d) = (3,1) allowed; c4 (d,a) = (1,1) allowed (since (1,1) is allowed). So component c1 is consistent.\n\nNow we have constraints linking to other components: c17 (b,e) already used: b=0, e ∈ {2,3}. c20 (n,a): a=1, so (n,1) must be allowed. c20 allowed pairs: (2,1) or (3,3). So (n,a) = (n,1) must be (2,1) because a=1. So n must be 2. So n=2. That also satisfies c20: (2,1) is allowed.\n\nThus n=2.\n\nNow c13: (m,n) allowed: n=2, so (m,2) must be allowed. List c13 allowed pairs: (3,2), (2,2), (3,3), (1,1), (0,2), (1,2). So we need second component 2. Which pairs have second component 2? (3,2) => m=3; (2,2) => m=2; (0,2) => m=0; (1,2) => m=1. So m ∈ {0,1,2,3}? Actually all four values appear: 3,2,0,1. So m can be 0,1,2,3. So m is unrestricted by c13 given n=2.\n\nNow c14: (n,o) allowed: n=2, so (2,o) must be allowed. List c14 allowed: (2,3), (3,2), (1,0), (1,2), (0,3), (2,2). So (2,o) appears for o=3 (2,3) and o=2 (2,2). So o ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus o ∈ {2,3}.\n\nNow c15: (o,p) allowed: depends on o.\n\nc16: (p,m) allowed: depends on p and m.\n\nNow component c2: e,f,g,h.\n\nWe have b=0, c17: (b,e) = (0,e) must be allowed: allowed pairs (0,2) or (0,3). So e ∈ {2,3} as earlier.\n\nThus e ∈ {2,3}.\n\nNow c5: (e,f) allowed: depends on e.\n\nc5 allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,2), (3,1), (1,3), (1,0), (2,0). So for each e, possible f values:\n\n- If e=2: allowed (2,2) => f=2; (2,0) => f=0. So f ∈ {0,2} when e=2.\n\n- If e=3: allowed (3,1) => f=1. No other with e=3? Check list: (3,1) only. So if e=3, f must be 1.\n\nThus possibilities:\n\nCase 1: e=2, f ∈ {0,2}.\n\nCase 2: e=3, f=1.\n\nNow c6: (f,g) allowed: depends on f.\n\nc6 allowed pairs: (2,2), (1,1), (0,0), (1,3), (0,2), (0,3). So for each f:\n\n- If f=0: allowed (0,0) => g=0; (0,2) => g=2; (0,3) => g=3. So g ∈ {0,2,3}.\n\n- If f=1: allowed (1,1) => g=1; (1,3) => g=3. So g ∈ {1,3}.\n\n- If f=2: allowed (2,2) => g=2. Also maybe (2,?) other? No other with first component 2. So g=2.\n\nThus g possibilities depend on f.\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed: depends on g.\n\nc7 allowed pairs: (2,0), (3,2), (1,1), (2,1), (1,3), (3,1). So for each g:\n\n- If g=0: no allowed pair with first component 0. So g cannot be 0. So any assignment with g=0 is invalid. So we must avoid g=0.\n\n- If g=1: allowed (1,1) => h=1; (1,3) => h=3. So h ∈ {1,3}.\n\n- If g=2: allowed (2,0) => h=0; (2,1) => h=1. So h ∈ {0,1}.\n\n- If g=3: allowed (3,2) => h=2; (3,1) => h=1. So h ∈ {1,2}.\n\nThus g cannot be 0; possible g values are 1,2,3.\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed: depends on h and e.\n\nc8 allowed pairs: (0,2), (1,0), (1,3), (2,3), (3,0), (2,1). So for each e, possible h values:\n\n- If e=2: allowed pairs with second component 2? Actually (h,e) means first is h, second is e. So we need (h,2) in allowed list. Check allowed list:\n\n(0,2) => h=0.\n(1,0) => e=0 not relevant.\n(1,3) => e=3 not relevant.\n(2,3) => e=3.\n(3,0) => e=0.\n(2,1) => e=1.\n\nThus only (0,2) has e=2. So if e=2, h must be 0.\n\n- If e=3: allowed pairs with e=3: (1,3) => h=1; (2,3) => h=2. So if e=3, h ∈ {1,2}.\n\nThus h possibilities:\n\n- If e=2: h=0.\n- If e=3: h ∈ {1,2}.\n\nNow combine with earlier constraints on h from c7.\n\nThus we need to satisfy both c7 (g->h) and c8 (h,e) constraints.\n\nThus we can enumerate possibilities for e,f,g,h.\n\nWe also have cross constraints linking f to i (c18) and e to b (c17 already used). So f determines i via c18. And i then participates in component c3 constraints (i,j,k,l). So f influences i.\n\nThus we need to consider f possibilities.\n\nThus let's enumerate possibilities for e,f,g,h systematically.\n\nWe have two cases for e:\n\nCase A: e=2.\n\n- Then b=0, c17 satisfied (0,2) allowed.\n\n- c5: (e,f) allowed: e=2 => f ∈ {0,2}.\n\n- c8: h must be 0 (since e=2 => h=0). So h=0.\n\n- c7: (g,h) = (g,0) must be allowed. Allowed pairs with second component 0: from c7 list: (2,0) => g=2, h=0; (3,2) not; (1,1) not; (2,1) not; (1,3) not; (3,1) not. So only (2,0) is allowed for (g,h) with h=0. So g must be 2. So g=2.\n\nThus for e=2, we have forced: h=0, g=2.\n\nNow c6: (f,g) = (f,2) must be allowed. c6 allowed pairs: (2,2) => f=2; (1,3) => g=3; (0,2) => f=0,g=2; (0,3) => g=3; (1,1) => g=1; (0,0) => g=0. So only (0,2) and (2,2) have second component 2. So f can be 0 (if (0,2)) or 2 (if (2,2)). Both are allowed.\n\nThus f ∈ {0,2} as already from c5.\n\nThus for e=2, we have:\n\n- h=0, g=2.\n\n- f ∈ {0,2}.\n\n- e=2.\n\nThus possible (e,f,g,h) combos:\n\n1. (2,0,2,0)\n2. (2,2,2,0)\n\nNow we need to check other constraints: c8 is satisfied (h=0,e=2) allowed (0,2). c7 satisfied (g=2,h=0) allowed (2,0). c6 satisfied for f choices: (0,2) allowed; (2,2) allowed. c5 satisfied for e=2: (2,0) allowed; (2,2) allowed. c17 satisfied (b=0,e=2) allowed. So component c2 constraints are satisfied for both combos.\n\nNow we need to ensure cross constraints linking f to i (c18) and other components can be satisfied.\n\nc18: (f,i) allowed: if f=0, then (0,i) must be allowed. c18 allowed pairs: (2,0) and (3,1). So (0,i) is not allowed for any i (since only (2,0) and (3,1)). So f cannot be 0 because (0,i) not allowed. Therefore f must be 2. So f=2 is forced.\n\nThus for e=2 case, we must have f=2. So (e,f,g,h) = (2,2,2,0) is the only viable tuple.\n\nThus one tuple for c2: (e=2, f=2, g=2, h=0).\n\nNow check c18: f=2 => i=0 (since (2,0) allowed). So i=0.\n\nThus i is forced to 0.\n\nNow we need to satisfy component c3 constraints: i,j,k,l with i=0.\n\nComponent c3 constraints:\n\nc9: (i,j) allowed: i=0 => (0,j) must be allowed. c9 allowed pairs: (0,2), (2,0), (0,3), (3,2), (2,1), (2,3). So (0,j) appears for j=2 (0,2) and j=3 (0,3). So j ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus j ∈ {2,3}.\n\nc10: (j,k) allowed: depends on j.\n\nc10 allowed pairs: (2,3), (0,0), (1,1), (2,2), (1,3), (3,0). So for each possible j:\n\n- If j=2: allowed (2,3) => k=3; (2,2) => k=2. So k ∈ {2,3}.\n\n- If j=3: allowed (3,0) => k=0. Also maybe (j,k) = (3,?) other? No other with first component 3. So if j=3, k=0.\n\nThus possibilities:\n\n- j=2 => k ∈ {2,3}\n- j=3 => k=0\n\nc11: (k,l) allowed: depends on k.\n\nc11 allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,3), (1,2), (1,0), (3,1), (3,3). So for each possible k:\n\n- If k=2: no pair with first component 2. So k cannot be 2. So j cannot be 2 with k=2? Actually we need to consider that k must be allowed with l; if k=2, there is no allowed (2,l). So k cannot be 2. So if j=2, k must be 3 (since k ∈ {2,3} but 2 invalid). So j=2 => k=3.\n\n- If k=3: allowed (3,0) => l=0; (3,1) => l=1; (3,3) => l=3. So l ∈ {0,1,3}.\n\n- If k=0: allowed (0,3) => l=3. So